SM(501,{"n":"Romans 1 Continued","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 1 Continued.mp3","d":3163.0,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.76,"In just a moment, we'll be picking up in uh St."],[25.36,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, chapter one, uh, verse eight."],[33.6,"So uh the reason we didn't get that far in Romans last time is that we had the introduction to the whole book last time, which uh at some time in the future for those sitting here with me, uh, but already for those people who might be listening to this recording later, we'll be available uh online uh to listen to."],[58.32,"Uh if you want to go back and hear that introduction, uh I'll do a little bit of catch-up."],[59.92,"Yes It shouldn't be that much catch-up because we only covered seven verses."],[66.04,"So but uh so we talked about just in brief last time that uh St."],[71.8,"Paul is writing uh this epistle, this letter, to uh the Christian community in Rome."],[79.64,"He's writing it in the mid-50s AD, probably between 54 and 56, uh so right in the middle of the decade."],[88.28,"Uh and we need to keep in mind a few things that that means."],[94.36,"That means that this is, if we take 56, we take the latest date."],[99.26,"That this is about 23 years after Christ's resurrection in the day of Pentecost."],[104.22,"So on the outside, this Christian community in Rome has existed for maybe 20 years"],[111.46,"So this is this is a nascent Christian community."],[114.92,"It's still sort of forming."],[116.92,"And as we talked about last time, you know, the there are not two separate religions of Judaism over here and Christianity over there."],[128.28,"But uh the Christian community is still a part of the synagogue community at this point in history."],[137.16,"So uh Christians, uh, people who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah, as the Christ, are for the most part going to the synagogues, whether they're people of Jewish ethnicity who have now accepted Jesus as the Messiah, or whether they're Gentiles, non Jews who have embraced Jesus as the Messiah."],[162.88,"They're going on on Saturday, on the Sabbath, to the synagogue for reading and preaching, though there's now Christian preaching going on in many of these synagogues."],[173.2,"And then on Sunday, on the first day of the week, they're gathering just as a Christian community the, as we talked about last time in Rome, Emperor Claudius expelled all the Jews from Rome."],[194.28,"And again, he didn't discriminate between Jews and Christian Jews."],[201.53,"But Gentiles who had joined the Christian community were not Jews."],[207.05,"And so you not only have this major segment of the nascent Christian community all thrown out of the city, uh to leave the Gentile community to carry on, but no Jewish community in Rome means no synagogues operating."],[224.8,"So for a period of between a year and two years, the the Gentile Christian community had to adjust to sort of functioning on its own."],[234.4,"And then after that year and a half or two years, they're allowed the Jewish community is allowed to return."],[240.94,"And so now we have this sort of reassimilation issue."],[245.98,"Why did Claudius do that for such a brief period of time?"],[250.14,"Um he actually did it, most people think at least twice."],[254.06,"Uh-huh. Uh that's and it's mainly because the the the Jews, as the in the same way that Nero would later use Christians as a scapegoat, the Jews were a convenient scapegoat uh because of their worship practices, specifically their refusal to worship the Roman gods."],[273.9,"And so when things weren't going right."],[277.76,"Well, why is it?"],[278.72,"Well, the gods must be unhappy with us?"],[280.44,"Well, why would they be unhappy with us?"],[282.4,"We've been doing the sacrifices like we're supposed to await these people living in our midst aren't doing the sacrifices like they're supposed to."],[291.68,"Well, then why would he not keep it up?"],[294.32,"Why would he invite them back a year later?"],[296.56,"Things would sort of cool off."],[299.36,"And it wasn't that they were invited back, it was more that the restriction just sort of ended."],[306.32,"Sort of passively, you know, sort of ended."],[309.2,"And there were a lot of reasons to want to be in Rome with it being the imperial capital."],[313.73,"So if people were allowed to return, they would return."],[317.57,"Um yeah, it's uh it's an odd dynamic, you know, from our perspective, especially."],[324.85,"Not that we don't have political scapegoating today."],[326.53,"We certainly do"],[330.01,"But in this in this case, you know, it would you have to remember there's no separation of church and state."],[336.17,"You know, all these things, religion, politics, the state, the culture, the society, are all woven together."],[344.57,"And uh, so Jews to a certain extent, and then Christians to an even larger extent, uh, are sort of opting out of all of that by refusing to participate in these things."],[359.29,"Um the the Romans, for the most part, not entirely, as obviously with this expulsion, but for the most part, kind of got a little bit of a pass because the Romans. acknowledged how ancient their beliefs were"],[376.21,"And in addition to that, they could kind of fit the Jewish beliefs into their religious logic in the sense that one of the biggest functions in terms of what gods you worshipped."],[390.85,"We tend to think, we read the Greek Pantheon or the Roman Pantheon, most Romans and Greeks did uh worshiped one or two particular gods."],[402.85,"And which ones those were."],[405.83,"Not that they denied the other ones, you know, but that was based on where largely where they lived, where they were from, their familiess' history of worshiping some god or other."],[416.15,"So you'd have this mix, maybe three or four, you know"],[420.33,"But if they were visiting somewhere else, and that city was seen to be you know the province of some other some other god, then they feel free to go and you know, make offerings Artemis."],[433.52,"And so you you know, you would do that too."],[435.52,"It would it wasn't exclusive."],[437.6,"But they could kind of fit the Jews, they'd say, well, the Jews, they just have this one, and from the Roman perspective, well, he's the god of their people in their country."],[448.45,"And so uh there are instances of both Greeks and Romans, not the god fearers we've been talking about, who are actually pious, but sort of paying tribute here and there, or wanting. to at the Jewish temple, you know, just to kind of cover all their bases and to say, oh, well, this is the God of the Jews"],[466.57,"So they can kind of fit, well, this is the God of the Jews into their system to a certain extent."],[472.81,"Christianity to them becomes more of an issue because number one, they see it as new."],[481.35,"This thing that just popped up."],[484.79,"And uh number two, uh, in that they're worshiping Christ, there's there's no antiquity to it, and they're saying they're making the claim."],[494.55,"We talked about this a little bit uh in uh the prologue of the epistle here, where where Saint Well, we talked about how St."],[503.71,"Paul's saying that giving titles to to Jesus Christ, like Savior, Lord, these are titles of Caesar."],[512.87,"So the Christians are making these claims about Jesus Christ, that he not only that, oh well, he's their God, but that he is the only God."],[522.76,"And you know, St."],[523.88,"Paul's gonna say things in first, you know, all the gods of the nations are demons."],[528.76,"You know, so they're making a very a very different claim."],[533.61,"We're gonna see, especially in 1st and 2nd Corinthians and in Galatians a little bit, and Ephesians and Colossians, St."],[540.09,"Paul has this view that Christ has sort of defeated and destroyed the Roman gods, who he believes to be. demons"],[549.01,"So that's a very different kind of teaching than the way the Romans, what the Romans saw the Jews teaching."],[558.37,"The Romans could sort of sort of say, well, yeah, let them go worship their God, whatever makes them happy."],[563.65,"We don't really care."],[568.13,"Christians are making sort of different claims."],[571.97,"You know, with when a Jewish person said that they believed that someday their Messiah would come and they'd rule the world and all the Gentiles would be their slaves."],[579.41,"That the Romans thought that was funny and quaint."],[581.93,"You know? Like, yeah, sure."],[584.51,"So, yeah, so it was a different sort of dynamic."],[589.43,"People who had been up to that time worshiping the Roman gods."],[594.0,"And and the Jews are not proselytizing."],[596.48,"Yeah. They're happy to have those God fearers."],[598.88,"If you want to come to the synagogue and learn about the Jewish scripture and and that kind of thing, great."],[604.88,"But they're not going out and trying to convert people at this point at this point."],[613.68,"So yeah, all of those things are sort of factored in there."],[617.6,"But so there were there were occasional flare-up."],[620.0,"Nothing like the persecution of Christians that would flare up later by the Romans."],[624.16,"I mean, col we're talking right now, compare Claudius's expel expelling the Jews just from the city of Rome twice to what Nero does with Christians."],[633.2,"There's sort of no comparison in terms of persecution level"],[640.28,"But so St."],[641.0,"Paul sees with the Jewish community now returning, with the Gentile community having gotten used to sort of operating on its own and operating outside the context of the synagogue, he sees this danger of the two communities not being reintegrated."],[657.57,"Of there being sort of this, of there being three groups now: Jews who don't accept Jesus as the Messiah, Jews who do accept Jesus as the Messiah, and then these Gentile Christians over here."],[668.79,"And and so a lot of the argument that we're gonna see, even starting tonight, as we get into the the body of St."],[677.67,"Paul's epistle. is going to be talking about uh St"],[680.91,"Paul arguing that the position in Christ of the Jewish person and the Gentile person who has accepted Jesus as the Messiah, they're in the same position in all respects."],[695.47,"They were in the same position before the gospel was preached to them."],[700.35,"They're in the same position now that they've accepted it."],[705.33,"And received it with faith."],[707.97,"And so that there's not a difference."],[709.89,"That's going to be the major overriding theme."],[713.01,"And then within that, he's going to need to explain sort of a whole series of other questions that arise from that."],[719.67,"Well, then what what do all the promises mean in the Old Testament to the nation of Israel then?"],[726.05,"If we're all the same now."],[727.81,"Um how come if Jesus is the Jewish Messiah, how come so many of the Jews don't accept him?"],[734.06,"There's gonna be all these other questions that he's gonna have to deal with along the way in terms of trying to bring these bring this one community back together to being one community again."],[745.1,"Um and so last time we basically, as I said, we read sort of the prologue."],[748.86,"It's sort of the introduction and greeting."],[750.94,"We talked about some of the themes that were in there."],[754.3,"It's a little bit theologically dense."],[756.06,"We'll see that with all of St."],[757.02,"Paul's epistles, his introductions."],[758.7,"He packs a whole bunch into it."],[760.46,"And a lot of times, then what he does in the rest of the letter is sort of unpack those things and pull those out and explain them."],[770.26,"So unless we have any other questions or comments or anything, we'll go ahead and get started in St."],[779.3,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans chapter one, verse eight."],[785.94,"And he hopefully you can tell we're getting into the body of his letter now because he hopefully starts with first."],[794.0,"In the first place."],[806.12,"So he starts out by saying, and he's gonna acknowledge this further, but remember as we said, St."],[814.12,"Paul hasn't been to Rome yet."],[815.64,"He's gonna go there, but he hasn't been there yet."],[818.44,"So he's hearing about the church in Rome and what's going on there."],[822.2,"And he starts by saying most of what I hear is good."],[826.41,"That people talk about, you know, and you know, being the Christian community in Rome, it's gonna become much more dire under Nero in uh in a decade."],[837.85,"But um they're in the middle of the Imperial Capitol."],[844.47,"They're in relatively hostile already territory, in terms of what they're dealing with, um, and the temptations they're dealing with as a community, uh, being in the sort of the hub, the heart of Roman culture, and yet trying to remain apart from it."],[863.09,"Has Peter been to Rome yet?"],[865.65,"It is unclear."],[867.89,"Most likely not."],[871.01,"Um, it isn't recorded, you know, we read the book of Acts."],[876.53,"Uh huh. It doesn't say when exactly Saint Peter went there."],[880.93,"We know he's there uh in the 60s and is martyred there."],[886.13,"Uh-huh. And we know he writes uh 1st Peter, although we talked about what writing means."],[892.93,"We know he dictates the letter first first Peter from Rome."],[898.65,"Because he indicates so at the beginning."],[900.21,"So then you get into well, when exactly was First Peter written"],[905.93,"That doesn't really help you in terms of exactly it doesn't say what day it was."],[910.25,"You know, there's not a date at the top of the letter."],[912.57,"Um so we know he was there in the 60s."],[916.57,"Uh whether he had been there yet in the mid 50s, my my surmise would be probably not."],[922.33,"Uh huh. Uh, because when we do see him in the book of Acts surrounding St."],[926.01,"Paul's missionary journeys, he's usually somewhere in Palestine."],[931.29,"U-hhuh. I mean, we saw him in Joppa, we saw him in you know, some different places, but still within Palestine during that period."],[938.82,"Now, when St."],[939.54,"Paul gets back from his missionary journeys, we read about him going and seeing St."],[943.06,"James, and it St."],[944.02,"Peter isn't is conspicuously absent."],[944.74,"in Peter was Antioch."],[947.74,"We also know that St."],[951.26,"So we don't know where he was when he wasn't in Jerusalem when St."],[955.26,"Paul got back there."],[957.26,"So all that is to say we're not sure."],[960.14,"My surmise would be mid 50s, probably not."],[964.78,"I would think it would have some bearing on our understanding of this, because if if Peter's been there or is actually there, um, then I think Paul would perhaps say things somewhat differently, or refer to Peter or something."],[987.74,"Yeah. Well, I mean, you would probably refer to him."],[989.9,"Uh right Yeah."],[990.5,"Yeah. And we we know St."],[991.86,"Peter didn't establish that church."],[995.7,"That church was established by people who would have been there on the day of Pentecost and then gone back home and uh preached the gospel from there."],[1005.7,"So yeah, my surmise would be St."],[1007.94,"Peter probably showed up sometime in the late 50s."],[1010.9,"So after this, um, as did St."],[1015.78,"Paul. Of course, St."],[1016.98,"Peter went there voluntarily."],[1018.26,"St. Paul sort of went there voluntarily."],[1020.66,"He wanted to go there, but he went there in chains."],[1023.14,"So he starts by uh saying that he gives thanks to God for the fact that they're now remember that your faith is spoken of."],[1035.08,"Uh this is going to be very important later in in St."],[1039.0,"Paul's Epistle of the Romans."],[1040.76,"When we talk about faith, we're not talking about sort of spiritual fervor or excitement."],[1047.32,"We're talking about faith is about trust and loyalty."],[1051.34,"And faithfulness. So when he talks about their faith, he's talking about their loyalty to Christ and their trust in Christ."],[1059.76,"Which again is is in the city of Rome."],[1064.42,"It's not going to be easy."],[1065.38,"They're going to face a lot of conflicts in their everyday life that they might not face in some other places."],[1070.42,"Like Antioch. If they were in the middle of the Jewish community in Antioch and part of the Christian community in Antioch, it would be it was a different thing than being sort of in the heart of Rome."],[1085.18,"For God is my witness, whom I serve with my spirit in the gospel of his son, that without ceasing I make mention of you always in my prayers."],[1095.18,"So he says, I'm constantly praying for you."],[1098.54,"All this is because again, he hasn't been there."],[1101.1,"But I've heard good reports."],[1103.58,"I'm praying for for you and for this community."],[1109.58,"Making request if by some means, now at last, I may find a way in the will of God to come to you."],[1116.43,"So he he tips off."],[1119.13,"And we saw this in the book of Acts, that even before he was arrested, his goal was to get to Rome."],[1124.53,"So he says, Not only am I praying for you, but I'm praying that at some point I manage to get there to see you in person."],[1133.49,"For I long to see you, that I may impart to you some spiritual gift, so that you may be established."],[1140.37,"That is, that I may be encouraged together with you by the mutual faith, both of you and me."],[1148.61,"So he says, uh, I want to go there because I want to teach you, you know, I want to teach you more about the faith."],[1157.83,"Because again, this community' beens evangelized."],[1163.32,"But if an apostle hasn't been there yet. where their level of knowledge of these things, we don't know exactly where it is"],[1171.94,"So he says, I want to come there, instruct you further so that you could be established, so that a foundation, I make sure a foundation is laid, you know, to strengthen you."],[1181.46,"And that this will, and that doing that will benefit St."],[1185.7,"Paul also. Being with them and doing this."],[1191.39,"Now, I do not want you to be unaware, brethren, that I often planned to come to you, but was hindered until now, that I might have some fruit among you also, just as among the other Gentiles."],[1206.75,"So he says, uh, I've been wanting for a long time."],[1213.27,"I notice he refers to them as brethren."],[1215.11,"Remember, we saw in the book of Acts, this is sort of what Christians called each other."],[1221.03,"They called each other, brothers, as they would say they visit the brethren in."],[1225.89,"Um, and uh this is not just this is not just a friendly state, you know, brother so and so, sister so and so."],[1235.51,"Like a title."],[1236.87,"Um this this is calling someone your brother or your sister in the context of this culture, especially in Roman culture, uh, entailed with it obligations."],[1251.21,"This is some of the early Roman pagan writers we have writing about Christianity."],[1254.09,"And they actually phrase it this way"],[1255.17,"This is one of the things they think is really weird about Christianity."],[1261.65,"In Roman morality, you had this responsibility to your family."],[1268.13,"If someone in your family was poor, or suffering some misfortune, that was a shame to the whole family, because everyone said, Why aren't why aren't the other people in the family taking care of this person?"],[1280.0,"Why are, you know, uh that wasn't a shame to that person, it was a shame to the everybody else that they were letting that happen."],[1287.1,"And so there was this network of responsibility, that that entailed by being part of a family."],[1293.6,"And the Romans looked at Christians, and it wasn't just that they call each other brother. and sister"],[1298.28,"It was that they said these Christians extend that to everyone in their community, even people who aren't related to them."],[1308.04,"That's what they didn't understand."],[1310.2,"There'd be some person who was poor, you know, probably was suffering in some way, and these people who are no relation whatsoever are, you know, paying their bills, you know, feeding them, you know, clothing taking care of them, as if this was a family member."],[1325.66,"So they think that's strange that you would try to extend that out past your immediate family."],[1331.66,"Because immediate family, yeah, you're obligated, but why would I do that for, you know, who cares about them?"],[1338.06,"Well, the Christians did."],[1340.3,"So, so when we see this language of brother and sister, this is an important statement."],[1347.86,"By saying, you know, you're my brother or you're my brother in Christ, you're my sister in Christ."],[1352.5,"That's not just a polite thing."],[1354.58,"That's saying, I'm making a commitment to you that I'm gonna be there for you the way I would, my physical literal brother or sister."],[1368.26,"And so he wants he's wanted to come because he wants to, as he says, he wants to have some fruit among them just as among the other Gentiles."],[1378.98,"So he's saying converts."],[1383.06,"In our terms."],[1384.12,"Yeah. And this, this, you know, while we talked about Judaism and Christianity not being separate religions, Christianity and paganism are very separate religions."],[1392.01,"So this is these are our straight up converts if we're talking about pagans."],[1396.14,"And uh that language is important too because that's not the language he uses when he's talking about God fearers."],[1402.94,"Uh-huh. You would go to the same."],[1404.28,"This is talking about pagans."],[1406.62,"This is talking about just straight up Greeks, Romans, barbarians."],[1412.46,"Now, uh Rome, unlike a lot of the places where St."],[1415.58,"Paul went on his missionary journeys, I mean, he when he went to Athens, almost everyone there's a Greek."],[1420.08,"You know, you've got Greeks and you've got a few Jews, you know, in a little Jewish community, but it's mostly Greek."],[1426.08,"Rome, you've got people from all over the Mediterranean world."],[1431.0,"So you've got a Jewish community there, but you've got Romans, you've got Greeks, you've got Egyptians, you've got other North Africans, you've got uh people from barbarian tribes in Europe."],[1442.84,"You've got everybody coming and doing business in uh in Rome."],[1449.16,"So a vast variety of pagans from whom Saint Paul wants to to bring some to Christ."],[1458.28,"He says, I am a debtor both to Greeks and to barbarians, both to wise and to unwise."],[1464.04,"So as much as is in me, I'm ready to preach the gospel to you who are in Rome also."],[1473.16,"So he's saying he doesn't discriminate."],[1488.24,"Comes from the fact that Greeks thought that all languages other than Greek sounded uh stupid."],[1496.8,"And so they said people who didn't speak Greek sounded like bar, bar, bar, bar, bar, bar, bar."],[1502.48,"That's where it comes from."],[1503.84,"Sort of like the adults on Charlie Brown."],[1506.48,"You know? So when when St."],[1511.28,"Paul says Greeks and barbarians, wise and unwise, those are parallel."],[1517.06,"To be Greek Greek and to speak was to be educated."],[1517.46,"And if you didn't and you spoke one of these other languages you were a barbarian."],[1523.4,"You were uneducated, uncultured."],[1530.09,"Okay. So he now wants to get there to Rome and preach the gospel to the Greeks and to the barbarians."],[1537.93,"Without distinction. Romans were not considered barbarians."],[1542.73,"No, but educated Romans all spoke Greek."],[1545.13,"Yeah. We have to remember, uh we think of Romans as speaking Latin."],[1551.87,"That that was their language, and Latin was used in the Senate."],[1556.19,"It was used by the emperor for official proclamations and this sort of thing."],[1560.27,"But day to day, even in the city of Rome, most people were speaking Greek because of this big mix of people from all over the world."],[1570.39,"It's sort of like if you go to a big city in Europe today and you have people from a whole bunch of different countries, you're liable to find them all speaking English to each other."],[1579.23,"You know, because it's sort of the bridge language, even if most of them it's not their first language, they've picked it up as a second or third language."],[1586.03,"So the same thing with Greek at this time in history."],[1590.67,"Unless you're a highly educated Roman, you wouldn't know any Latin."],[1595.07,"Um but people everywhere learned Greek."],[1599.65,"People in Palestine, their first language was Aramaic, but they learned Greek to be able to interact with everybody else."],[1604.85,"Okay So now, now that he's done that, he sort of has the prologue and his greeting, and then we have this sort of introduction."],[1619.31,"And we're gonna find, remember, this is a letter."],[1621.81,"So this kind of thing is normal."],[1623.75,"When you're writing a letter to someone."],[1625.27,"We're gonna find at the end of all of St."],[1627.11,"Paul's epistles. There's this whole there's this section which is always sort of say hi to so and so, say hi to so and so who's here with me says hi."],[1639.51,"So this is this beginning, is sort of the, you know, especially with a community like the one in Rome where he hasn't been there."],[1646.63,"When he writes to the Corinthians, okay, he's the one who helped establish the church in Corinth."],[1652.84,"So he here he's kind of saying, you know, no, I I know I have been there."],[1656.52,"I'm hoping to get there."],[1658.28,"I've been hoping to get there."],[1659.72,"I'm praying for you, and I'm praying that I get there."],[1662.22,"So he's sort of introducing it."],[1663.74,"Now, this is we're gonna sort of get into the meat."],[1667.42,"Why does he say he's a debtor to Greeks and barbarians?"],[1672.22,"Uh because he's trying to make the point that he doesn't discriminate between them, that both of them have assisted him and helped him."],[1681.9,"Okay. That he's not uh remember, remember what the issue primary issues we're dealing with is this sort of ethnic issue."],[1693.46,"And so he's trying to make the point that that's not an issue for him."],[1698.74,"That people have all kinds of ethnicities."],[1702.08,"Whether they're Jewish, whether they're Greek, whether they're barbarians, whether they're Scythians, whether they're whatever."],[1708.04,"That he all of them have assisted him, all of them have helped him."],[1710.92,"He's thankful for all of them."],[1713.18,"And that's kind of a subtle thing."],[1714.96,"Because it's not directly addressing the problem he's going to be addressing, but it's in the right, it's in the ballpark."],[1726.0,"And remember, he's also just said for what we're about to start, he's talking about going there and preaching the gospel, and we talked about what that means."],[1732.32,"We sort of redefined that last time."],[1734.12,"That this is he's coming to proclaim the victory of Christ."],[1734.88,"He says, For I'm not ashamed of the gospel of Christ"],[1742.52,"For it is the power of God to salvation for everyone who believes, for the Jew first and also for the Greek."],[1752.44,"So he says, I'm not embarrassed about I'll come and preach it to anybody."],[1757.06,"I'm not shy."],[1759.52,"He will preach it to anyone."],[1761.28,"Because it's the power of God."],[1765.28,"And that to salvation is uh it's actually an unto."],[1769.44,"It's uh unto meaning the purpose of it, what it does."],[1772.73,"The gospel, when it's preached, has the power to to save, to bring salvation. to everyone who believes it"],[1781.41,"This uh let me ask about beliefs here, because um in modern context, so often uh Christian faith is re reduced to belief in the sense of assenting to certain propositions."],[1799.27,"The true false test."],[1800.39,"Yes. Um what's the sense in which he's using it here?"],[1806.44,"Well that that is required as a first level."],[1808.84,"Sure. Uh huh."],[1809.7,"When St. Paul comes and preaches the gospel to you and tells you that Jesus is the Messiah, he was crucified, on the third day he rose again, he ascended into heaven, sits at the right hand of God, and he's going to come again to judge the living and the dead."],[1821.86,"But that's not it"],[1822.0,"So first you have to believe that that's true."],[1824.08,"Uh-huh. So that is the basic layer."],[1826.72,"Uh-huh. It's not just ascenting to right true on the true false test."],[1832.61,"Remember, we're just talking about the the word faith, the noun faith, and the verb believe are the same word in Greek."],[1839.81,"Oh, really? So St."],[1841.09,"Paul's using the same word here."],[1843.89,"Oh. Faith is pistis."],[1847.49,"Yeah, is pistes, and the verb is pistevo."],[1851.01,"It's the same."],[1853.93,"sam Samee root."],[1854.93,"Uh-hhu. Same root."],[1856.47,"And so you could translate them."],[1859.19,"You could translate where it says faith as belief, or you could translate believes as faiths."],[1865.27,"Uh-huh Um so the person who believes in it is not only accepting the truth of it, though that is implied."],[1872.3,"But they're also putting their trust and their confidence and their loyalty and their loyalty to right."],[1878.3,"It's the gospel of Christ, so that's loyalty to Christ."],[1881.59,"So you're accepting that Christ is the one who's the Lord of heaven and earth."],[1886.96,"And he's returning to judge the living and the dead."],[1889.84,"And so that then has consequences."],[1891.76,"Yeah, it does it does include then the consequences."],[1894.96,"It doesn't mean, oh, that's true."],[1898.0,"Interesting. And that's gonna become important later on because, as we mentioned last time, our Protestant friends like to separate out faith and works."],[1909.05,"Like they're these two different things."],[1911.69,"And, you know, if uh the doctor came to you tomorrow and said, look, you have six months to live."],[1920.71,"And you went out and put a bunch of money into long term investments."],[1925.84,"People would look at you and say, I don't think he believed the doctor."],[1930.16,"Uh huh. Not because you said, I don't believe I'm gonna die in six months, but because your actions imply right that you don't actually think you're gonna you're gonna die in six months."],[1945.79,"And so the same is true in this case."],[1948.53,"If you really believe that Jesus Christ is now reigning over all creation from heaven and is the Lord of the Lord of all, and he's going to return at some point in the imminent future to judge the living and the dead."],[1965.18,"That, if you really believe that, that will affect then how you live your whole life."],[1974.1,"So you can't sort of strip out the activity involved in loyalty and faithfulness and trust."],[1985.54,"And St Those the they're they go together."],[1988.98,"You can't separate those two out as two different things."],[1992.1,"Paul doesn't do that."],[1992.98,"But when we get to that, we'll talk more about what St."],[1995.86,"Paul is doing with that."],[1997.62,"But he's not separating these two out."],[2001.7,"He's not saying, Well, I'm gonna come and I'm gonna tell you who Jesus is."],[2006.34,"And if you say, yeah, I believe it, then you're saved."],[2009.94,"That's not what he's saying here."],[2013.88,"But he's saying that Well, that's that's why I asked the question."],[2017.7,"It's the power it's the power of God unto salvation for everyone who believes."],[2023.54,"So the verb tenses here are important."],[2025.38,"Believes is in the present tense, it's ongoing."],[2027.94,"Uh-huh. Not believed."],[2030.47,"At one point in time."],[2031.67,"There's a different Greek verb tense if you want to say that."],[2035.11,"You did it"],[2035.23,"Okay, there's a Greek verb tense called the eras tense."],[2038.03,"That's for an action that's completed, meaning it's in the past."],[2042.51,"Been there, done that."],[2043.65,"And the present tense in Greek is ongoing."],[2047.23,"So this is everyone who believes ongoing."],[2051.31,"And the gospel is the power of God unto salvation for the person who believes it."],[2057.31,"So salvation comes at the end through faith."],[2065.23,"It's not all past tense."],[2067.53,"Now I'm saved because I believed at some at some point."],[2077.05,"And you notice now he's starting to get into his theme a little bit."],[2079.37,"Okay For the Jew first and also for the Greek."],[2086.26,"Now here I have to make an important point."],[2088.34,"This isn't just talking about his strategy."],[2090.58,"We saw that in in the book of Acts, when Saint Paul went to a new city, he went to the synagogue first and preached there."],[2098.03,"And then after he preached at the synagogue, usually after he got thrown out of the synagogue, he went to the people, the regular run of the mill pagan Gentiles, outside."],[2109.57,"Outside of the synagogue."],[2111.17,"He's not just referring to that here, when he says to the Jew first and then to the Greek."],[2116.37,"He's speaking historically."],[2119.19,"He's speaking historically."],[2120.99,"Historically, the gospel was proclaimed first to Jews and then to the Greeks."],[2128.76,"Because when Jesus Christ became incarnate, he becomes incarnate in Judea."],[2134.1,"Remember the word Jew here is really the Greek word for Judeans."],[2139.46,"So yeah, the Judeans had him living in their midst."],[2144.03,"So they saw Christ first."],[2145.63,"They heard Christ preaching first."],[2148.33,"He rose from the dead right there outside Jerusalem."],[2152.53,"So So they had first."],[2154.87,"And then the gospel went out to the Greeks, to the rest of the to the rest of the world."],[2162.75,"And it's important to make that point here, not because that's a big thing here, but Saint Paul is going to speak historically through most of his epistle to the Romans."],[2175.51,"And this is one of the big errors in interpreting the epistle to the Romans, is not understanding that Saint Paul is talking about the history of Israel and the Jewish people and the Gentiles, thinking he's talking about like things I experience in my own life, for example."],[2196.47,"He's speaking historically here, and he's going to continue to speak historically."],[2205.07,"So it doesn't mean primarily to the Jews and also secondarily to the Jews."],[2211.81,"Right. This is a historical sequence."],[2216.34,"And, you know, you see that in the Great Commission at the end of St."],[2220.42,"Matthew's gospel. Obviously, all of the apostles there are all Jews."],[2226.67,"They're all Judeans."],[2228.51,"But Christ says that go out and make disciples from all of the nations."],[2236.27,"Baptizing them in the name of the Father, Son, and the Holy Spirit."],[2242.19,"And so that's that's also how we understand what St."],[2246.59,"Paul, the principle that St."],[2248.11,"Paul's talking about there historically is also how we understand a whole bunch of passages in the Gospels."],[2252.75,"Like when Christ tells St"],[2254.47,"Fotini at the at the well in Samaria that salvation is of the Jews."],[2260.21,"Or is from the Jews."],[2261.57,"That's what he means."],[2263.19,"Because he's a Judean, a Galilean, technically."],[2268.79,"But he's right."],[2270.07,"But he's, you know, that first, and then it goes out to the whole world."],[2274.31,"Um that's what when Christ tells uh the Canaanite woman."],[2280.73,"The or the Syrophoenician woman, depending on what translation you have, that uh he's come only to the twelve tribes of Israel."],[2289.66,"Um, that's how to understand all of those things."],[2292.62,"There are sort of these two phases."],[2294.55,"Because Jesus is promised as the Messiah to Israel."],[2301.81,"To the Jewish people by the prophets."],[2303.73,"And so he comes to them and fulfills that promise."],[2306.85,"But then from there it moves out to encompass the whole world."],[2315.17,"For in it, meaning in the gospel, the righteousness of God is revealed from faith to faith, as it is written, the just shall live by faith."],[2328.77,"You have to work hard. on that"],[2330.37,"Yeah. Well, this is this is gonna be another one of those problems with words being translated differently."],[2336.29,"Okay. So the word there, the just and the word righteousness, is the same word."],[2348.09,"Yeah, in Arabic it's the same word."],[2349.53,"Yeah, it's the same word in Greek."],[2353.05,"The word uh decaos uh can mean uh really mean anywhere you see it outside of the New Testament, it's translated as justice."],[2363.83,"Plato's Republic, the whole dialogue is about what is the nature of decaus?"],[2370.39,"What is justice?"],[2374.95,"And uh there are a couple of forms of it that occur here."],[2380.71,"Uh uh dicaosini, which is why it's translated as righteousness, justiceness isn't as doesn't roll off the justness, I guess."],[2395.39,"But they're the same word."],[2397.55,"Okay. And you also notice that St."],[2403.23,"Paul quotes Habakkuk."],[2406.35,"So that's the italics there's Habakkuk."],[2411.05,"Habakkuk chapter 2, verse 4."],[2413.61,"So I haven't done a lot of this in the past, but I'm going to do this in St."],[2418.41,"Paul's epistles, and I'm going to do it for a reason."],[2422.57,"Because this is another way that people misinterpret St."],[2429.13,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans a lot of times, is that they treat his quotes from the Old Testament like sort of proof texts or something and gloss over them."],[2440.85,"But when St."],[2441.97,"Paul quotes something from the Old Testament or from his perspective from the scriptures, he's trying to bring some ideas that are actually there in that text to bear."],[2456.21,"He expects them."],[2457.33,"We've talked before about how they didn't have chapters and verses."],[2461.35,"At this point, so St."],[2462.79,"Paul couldn't say, well, it's like it says in Habakkuk chapter 2, verses 4 through 12."],[2469.84,"He couldn't say it."],[2470.72,"So you quote the first line, and you he expected, because of their familiarity with the scriptures, that when he quoted that first line, that would call to mind what's going on in the context."],[2484.68,"So I'm actually gonna go back to Hobak Habakkuk uh two and uh and uh read it read a little bit."],[2508.2,"Okay, so uh I'm gonna start uh in verse one of Habakkuk II."],[2514.12,"It says I'll stand on my watch, mounted upon the rock, and see. what he shall say to me, and what might I answer when I am reproved"],[2524.48,"And the Lord answered me and said, Write the vision distinctly upon a tablet, that he who reads it may flee."],[2531.6,"For the vision is yet for an appointed time, and it will come up at the end, and it will not be in vain."],[2537.92,"For he should if he should tarry, wait for him, for he will surely come and he will not tarry."],[2543.2,"I know you're not understanding."],[2548.84,"Man should shrink back, my soul will not be well pleased in him, but the righteous shall live by my faith."],[2554.84,"But the one who is drunk and the scoffer, the man who boasts, shall accomplish nothing."],[2561.32,"He wind his soul as the grave, and as death he is never satisfied."],[2565.8,"And he will gather to himself all the nations, and will take to himself all the peoples."],[2572.76,"So we'll pause there."],[2575.16,"Do you notice especially that last verse?"],[2578.12,"This is God's plan to bring in all the people."],[2581.21,"This quote from Habakkuk is important because St."],[2583.93,"Paul here establishes a bunch of themes that we're going to see later through the whole epistle."],[2590.49,"Okay, so he talks about that beginning part, just to explain it briefly."],[2594.95,"I'm not going to do a Bible study on Habakkuk now, but but just to explain it briefly."],[2599.43,"Habakkuk has this vision."],[2602.59,"And God tells him, Look, you need to take this vision and write it down, because this vision is about something that's going to happen later."],[2610.91,"That's what it means by it's going to be for the end."],[2613.76,"So this isn't something, I'm not giving you this vision because this is something that's going to happen in your own life, Habakkuk, but you need to write this down and pass it down."],[2621.76,"So part of what St."],[2622.8,"Paul is doing by quoting this is saying, That time is now."],[2626.8,"That time has now come."],[2628.24,"This time that was prophesied before."],[2632.08,"And then what he says here has has two parts."],[2635.2,"The first part is, and the part that he quotes is from, if any man should shrink back, my soul will not be well pleased in him, but the righteous shall live by my faith."],[2646.6,"Okay, so there's a contrast here."],[2648.64,"There's a righteous person who is faithful to God, and there's another person who shrinks back."],[2650.88,"God. Who shrinks back, and is not pleased with that person"],[2658.1,"Okay. So right away here, when Martin Luther quotes this verse, he says, Oh, see, the righteous will live by faith, not works."],[2669.38,"Yeah, this is the big Lutheran."],[2671.59,"By faith, not works."],[2673.83,"But how does Habakkuk go on to describe the person who shrinks back?"],[2681.27,"The one who is drunk and the scoffer, the man who boasts."],[2686.37,"These are behaviors."],[2687.61,"are these These are behaviors."],[2689.25,"These are sinful behaviors."],[2692.02,"So living by faith means living righteously."],[2697.04,"That's what Habakkuk is very clearly saying here."],[2700.85,"And the time when this is going to happen, the time when this sifting out between the righteous who will be faithful and the unrighteous who will be sinners, this is going to happen at the end."],[2711.02,"And when that happens, when that happens, that is when God is going to gather to himself all the nations and take to himself all the peoples."],[2724.22,"Okay So that first group."],[2729.35,"Amongst whom you have the righteous who are faithful, and you have those who shrink back who are living in a sinful way."],[2737.67,"If this is before he's called the Gentiles of the nations to themselves, who is it?"],[2746.31,"It's the people of Israel."],[2747.59,"It's the, or specifically the Judeans."],[2750.44,"The Jews. Some of them are going to be faithful and live in faithfulness to God."],[2758.28,"Some of them are going to what?"],[2761.4,"Shrink back and fall away."],[2763.14,"And when they shrink back and fall away, what is God going to do?"],[2768.86,"He's going to bring in people from all the nations and join them to those, that righteous, faithful remnant."],[2780.94,"So when St."],[2781.9,"Paul quotes this, he's not just proof to anything, he's quoting that whole idea."],[2786.22,"And we're going to see, he's going to keep coming back to that idea all through Romans."],[2791.92,"That there's been this judgment that's happened among the Jewish people."],[2797.84,"And that's what is represented by those who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah and those who haven't."],[2803.76,"The righteous have."],[2806.24,"And that the righteous have faith."],[2808.56,"The unrighteous have shrunk back and fallen away."],[2811.44,"And now God has called in the people from the nations to live faithfully and to replace them who have fallen away."],[2821.84,"So it actually means almost the exact opposite. of how Luther interpreted it"],[2828.77,"So this may be less important, but it's St."],[2834.93,"Paul can be a confusing writer."],[2837.17,"And when he says it's revealed from faith to faith, what does that mean?"],[2843.67,"Because what he's saying is those people, we talked about this a little bit in St."],[2849.59,"John's Gospel. Remember, there are people who recognize Jesus and people who don't recognize Jesus."],[2854.45,"And Jesus says, for example, you don't hear my voice because you're not my sheep."],[2858.47,"Or if you knew the Father, you would know me."],[2861.48,"But because you don't know me, that shows you don't know the Father either."],[2865.34,"It's that same kind of idea."],[2867.84,"So the people who were faithful, the Jewish people who are faithful to God, when they hear the gospel, who are the righteous, the faithful, this remnant, when they hear the gospel, they accept it in faith."],[2881.12,"So they move from faith to faith."],[2883.9,"Their faith continues."],[2885.02,"The faithfulness they had to God now extends to faithfulness to Jesus Christ."],[2890.62,"Okay. Uh huh."],[2891.52,"When they hear the gospel."],[2894.2,"And um. And so the ones who didn't weren't faithful originally, the people who shrank back in Habakkuk, they're the ones who don't.'t respond with faith in the gospel because they didn have any faith to begin with"],[2916.38,"That's a lot, three lines."],[2918.46,"Yeah. And part of part of it is again Saint Paul is writing to people who he expects to just oh yeah, Habakkuk on the watchtower."],[2930.62,"Maybe it's not somebody who was born on that line with wine."],[2934.38,"It was. It was."],[2935.74,"Because the these are they were hearing these texts in the same way that uh I just did it."],[2943.5,"Remember when I said St."],[2944.62,"Paul's here is interpreting stories like St."],[2947.18,"Fautini at the well."],[2949.1,"You knew what I was talking about."],[2950.22,"about. Well, because one Sunday every year we read that story and it's preached and it's talked"],[2951.54,"Why? Yeah. Same thing for them at this time going to the synagogue."],[2962.32,"They've heard Habakkuk in the cycle of readings every year, and they've heard this stuff preached."],[2969.62,"So they know it the way I can refer to things in the gospel."],[2974.42,"So that's why he can just sort of cite it."],[2978.1,"Okay, yeah. Okay."],[2981.75,"But there's even there's even some of this in our liturgics."],[2986.35,"Because for example, one of the odes of the canon in Matins is about Habakkuk, and it talks about Habakkuk on his watchtower all the time."],[2993.99,"And I think most folks are sort of like"],[3032.51,"Isaiah is big."],[3033.63,"Yeah, Isaiah's big stuff."],[3035.15,"Ezekiel is weird, so you don't get that quoted as much."],[3038.43,"Habakkuk is just ignored."],[3040.87,"But Isaiah in certain parts of Jeremiah, yeah."],[3043.19,"Well, yeah, all the except for Jonah."],[3045.35,"You usually read Jonah because it's got some interesting stories."],[3047.27,"But like I mean Obadiah, Joel, Anos, Hosea, Zephaniah, all of those from the twelve prophets are neglected a lot."],[3056.25,"Yeah. But I mean we don't go back and read these."],[3059.45,"I don't know why."],[3061.05,"I mean, they're just names."],[3062.41,"Yeah. And and what separates Habakkuk, the reason why you see more than say Obadiah."],[3071.37,"Uh-huh. If you read Obadiah, Obadiah is a series of oracles against the nation of Edom."],[3076.49,"And so it's important."],[3077.83,"But it was he was giving prophecies that were about things that were happening in his own period."],[3082.95,"And so there's of course things we learn from that that we can take from that"],[3087.55,"But Habakkuk, as we saw, is specifically, he's having a vision that's about something that hasn't happened yet."],[3095.82,"And so, especially not just the Christians, the Jews at this time read that as, oh, this is a prophecy about the Messianic Age, in the future."],[3104.84,"And so the early Christians were like, yes, this is a prophecy about now."],[3108.92,"And so Habakkuk, even out of those books, becomes very important to Christians for that reason."],[3118.04,"Because as we've talked about before, we tend to use prophet to mean someone who predicts the future."],[3124.12,"But that's not what it meant in the ancient world."],[3125.96,"It meant someone who speaks for God."],[3127.84,"And sometimes God would say things about the future, but sometimes he'd say things about the present, and sometimes he'd say things about the past."],[3137.2,"So it includes all of those in prophecy."],[3140.8,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[3147.92,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3153.28,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.76,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Romans' setting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Romans 1:8</b> - last time only <b>seven verses</b> (introduction to whole book)</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul writing to Christian community in <b>Rome</b>, mid-50s AD, probably <b>54-56</b></li><li class=\"d1\">latest date → ~<b>23 years after resurrection / Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">community maybe <b>20 years old</b> at most - <b>nascent</b>, still forming</li><li class=\"d0\">Not two religions yet: Christians still part of the <b>synagogue community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish and Gentile believers go <b>Saturday</b> to synagogue - reading and preaching, now <b>Christian preaching</b> in many</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sunday</b>, first day of the week: gather as Christians</li></ul>"},{"t":173.2,"lv":2,"h":"Claudius expels the Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Claudius</b> expelled all Jews from Rome - no distinction for <b>Christian Jews</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gentile Christians not Jews → stayed; Jewish segment gone, <b>no synagogues</b> operating</li><li class=\"d0\">Gentile community on its own <b>1-2 years</b>, then Jews allowed back → <b>reassimilation issue</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Done at least <b>twice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews = <b>convenient scapegoat</b> (as Christians later under <b>Nero</b>) - refused to worship Roman gods</li><li class=\"d1\">\"gods must be unhappy… those people aren't doing the sacrifices\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Return: restriction just <b>passively ended</b>; reasons to be in the <b>imperial capital</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No separation of church and state - religion, politics, culture <b>woven together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews, and Christians more, <b>opting out</b></li></ul>"},{"t":359.29,"lv":3,"h":"Why Rome tolerated Jews but not Christians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jews got a <b>pass</b> - Romans respected <b>antiquity</b> of the beliefs</li><li class=\"d0\">Fit into Roman logic: most people worshiped <b>one or two gods</b> by place and family history, not exclusive</li><li class=\"d1\">visiting a city → make offerings to its god (e.g. <b>Artemis</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">Jews: just have one - \"god of their people in their country\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some Greeks, Romans (not God-fearers) paid tribute at the <b>Jewish temple</b> to \"cover all their bases\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity: <b>new</b>, no antiquity</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Savior, Lord</b> = titles of <b>Caesar</b>; Christ claimed as the <b>only God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: all gods of the nations are <b>demons</b> (1-2 Corinthians, Galatians, Ephesians, Colossians) - Christ <b>defeated</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Jewish Messiah ruling world, Gentiles slaves → Romans found it <b>\"funny and quaint\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jews not <b>proselytizing</b> - happy to have God-fearers</li><li class=\"d0\">Claudius' expulsions = flare-ups, \"no comparison\" to Nero's <b>persecution</b></li></ul>"},{"t":640.28,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's concern: reintegrating the community","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Danger: <b>three groups</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews who <b>don't</b> accept Jesus</li><li class=\"d1\">Jews who <b>do</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gentile Christians</b> on their own</li><li class=\"d0\">Overriding theme: Jewish and Gentile believers in Christ are in the <b>same position in all respects</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same position <b>before</b> the gospel was preached, same <b>now</b> that received with faith</li><li class=\"d0\">Follow-on questions he must answer</li><li class=\"d1\">what do the <b>OT promises to Israel</b> mean?</li><li class=\"d1\">if Jesus is the <b>Jewish Messiah</b>, why do so many Jews not accept him?</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: bring <b>one community back together</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Intros are <b>theologically dense</b> - he packs it in, then <b>unpacks</b> in the letter</li></ul>"},{"t":779.3,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 1:8-10 - Thanks for your faith, constant prayer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:8</b>: now into the <b>body</b> of the letter - \"first\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul hasn't been to Rome - <b>hears</b> about the church: <b>mostly good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Rome = heart of Roman culture; <b>hostile</b>, temptations; far worse under <b>Nero</b> in a decade</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Has Peter been there?</b> unclear, most likely <b>not</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in Rome in the <b>60s</b>, martyred there; dictates <b>1 Peter</b> from Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">in Acts during Paul's journeys he's in <b>Palestine</b> (Joppa); <b>conspicuously absent</b> when Paul sees James</li><li class=\"d1\">surmise: arrived <b>late 50s</b>, after this - Paul would otherwise refer to him</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter did <b>not</b> found the church - Pentecost pilgrims did</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter went voluntarily; Paul \"went there <b>in chains</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith</b> = <b>trust, loyalty, faithfulness</b>, not spiritual fervor</li><li class=\"d1\">important later; loyalty to Christ in Rome harder than in <b>Antioch</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:9-10</b>: God my witness, <b>without ceasing</b> pray for you; hope to come in God's will</li><li class=\"d1\">Acts: goal to reach Rome even before arrest</li></ul>"},{"t":1133.49,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 1:11-13 - Longing to come, fruit among you","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:11-12</b>: impart <b>spiritual gift</b>, be <b>established</b>, mutually encouraged</li><li class=\"d1\">wants to <b>teach more about the faith</b> - evangelized, but no apostle yet; level of knowledge unknown</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lay a foundation</b>, strengthen - benefits Paul too</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:13</b>: often planned, <b>hindered</b> until now - fruit among you as among other Gentiles</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Brethren</b>\" - what Christians called each other (Acts)</li></ul>"},{"t":1225.89,"lv":2,"h":"Brother and sister: obligations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not a friendly title - in Roman culture, <b>obligations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Early pagan writers found it <b>weird</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Roman morality: poor or suffering relative = <b>shame on the whole family</b>, not the person</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>network of responsibility</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christians extended it to <b>everyone in the community</b>, no relation</li><li class=\"d1\">paying bills, feeding, clothing - \"as if a family member\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"My brother in Christ\" = <b>commitment</b> to be there as for a literal sibling</li></ul>"},{"t":1368.26,"lv":3,"h":"Fruit among the Gentiles: pagans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Fruit among other Gentiles\" = <b>converts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Judaism and Christianity not separate religions; <b>Christianity and paganism are</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the language used for <b>God-fearers</b> - this is <b>straight pagans</b>: Greeks, Romans, barbarians</li><li class=\"d0\">Rome unlike <b>Athens</b> (mostly Greek, small Jewish community)</li><li class=\"d1\">people from all over: Romans, Greeks, <b>Egyptians, North Africans</b>, barbarian tribes</li><li class=\"d1\">vast variety of pagans</li></ul>"},{"t":1458.28,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 1:14-15 - Debtor to Greeks and barbarians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:14</b>: \"debtor both to Greeks and to <b>barbarians</b>, wise and unwise\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Barbarian</b>: Greeks thought other languages sounded stupid - \"<b>bar, bar, bar</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like the adults on Charlie Brown\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Greeks / barbarians, wise / unwise = <b>parallel</b>: speaking Greek = <b>educated</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:15</b>: ready to preach to you in Rome - <b>without distinction</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Romans not barbarians?</li><li class=\"d1\">educated Romans all spoke <b>Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Latin</b>: Senate, emperor's proclamations; day to day, even in Rome, <b>Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like big European cities today - everyone speaks <b>English</b> as bridge language</li><li class=\"d1\">Palestine: first language <b>Aramaic</b>, learned Greek</li></ul>"},{"t":1604.85,"lv":2,"h":"Why \"debtor\"?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Prologue and greeting done → intro; letter ends with <b>\"say hi to so and so\"</b> in all epistles</li><li class=\"d0\">Rome different: he hasn't been there; <b>Corinth</b> he helped establish</li><li class=\"d0\">Debtor: <b>no discrimination</b> - all ethnicities have <b>assisted and helped</b> him</li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish, Greek, barbarian, <b>Scythian</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Subtle: not directly the <b>ethnic issue</b>, but \"in the ballpark\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Preaching the gospel = proclaiming <b>Christ's victory</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1734.88,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 1:16 - Not ashamed; power of God to salvation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:16</b>: \"not ashamed of the gospel of Christ… <b>power of God to salvation</b> for everyone who believes, for the Jew first and also for the Greek\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm not shy\" - will preach to <b>anyone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>unto</b> salvation\" = purpose, what it does</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>belief</b> - modern reduction to <b>assenting to propositions</b>, true/false test</li><li class=\"d1\">first level: must <b>believe it's true</b> - crucified, risen third day, ascended, <b>comes to judge</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but faith (<i>pistis</i>) and believe (<i>pisteuo</i>) = <b>same Greek root</b></li><li class=\"d1\">accepting truth <b>plus trust, confidence, loyalty</b> - loyalty to <b>Christ</b>, Lord of heaven and earth</li><li class=\"d1\">has <b>consequences</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1894.96,"lv":3,"h":"Faith and works not separable","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Protestants <b>separate faith and works</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Illustration: doctor says <b>six months to live</b>, you put money in <b>long-term investments</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ \"I don't think he believed the doctor\" - <b>actions</b> show it</li><li class=\"d0\">Really believe Christ <b>reigning now</b>, returning to judge → affects <b>whole life</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Can't strip out activity</b> from loyalty, faithfulness, trust - Paul doesn't</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"say you believe it and you're saved\" - more later</li></ul>"},{"t":2017.7,"lv":3,"h":"\"Everyone who believes\" - present tense","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Believes</b> = <b>present tense</b>, ongoing</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <b>aorist</b> = completed, past (\"been there, done that\")</li><li class=\"d1\">present = <b>ongoing</b>: everyone who <b>keeps believing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Salvation comes <b>at the end through faith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"now I'm saved because I believed at some point\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2077.05,"lv":2,"h":"\"The Jew first\": a historical sequence","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul starting on his theme</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts</b> strategy: synagogue first, then (usually thrown out) pagan Gentiles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> just that here - <b>speaking historically</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel proclaimed first to <b>Jews</b>, then Greeks</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ incarnate in <b>Judea</b>; \"Jew\" = Greek word for <b>Judeans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they saw and heard him first; rose outside <b>Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul speaks historically through most of Romans</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>big error</b>: thinking he's about <b>personal experience</b> rather than history of <b>Israel, Jews, Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not primary/secondary - a <b>sequence</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Great Commission</b> (Matthew): all apostles Judeans, told to disciple <b>all nations</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2252.75,"lv":2,"h":"Other texts: salvation is of the Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christ to the <b>Samaritan woman</b> at the well: <b>salvation is from the Jews</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he's a Judean (Galilean, technically); first, then out to the world</li><li class=\"d0\">To the <b>Canaanite / Syrophoenician woman</b>: come only to the <b>twelve tribes</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Two phases</b>: Messiah promised to Israel by prophets → fulfills it → moves out to the <b>whole world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2315.17,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 1:17 - Righteousness of God from faith to faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:17</b>: \"in it the <b>righteousness of God</b> is revealed from faith to faith, as it is written, <b>the just shall live by faith</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Translation problem: \"<b>the just</b>\" and \"<b>righteousness</b>\" = <b>same word</b> (<i>dikaios</i>, <i>dikaiosyne</i>)</li><li class=\"d1\">outside NT = <b>justice</b> - Plato's <i>Republic</i> is about <i>dikaiosyne</i></li><li class=\"d1\">\"justness\" doesn't roll off the tongue</li><li class=\"d1\">Arabic same word too</li><li class=\"d0\">Quote from <b>Habakkuk 2:4</b> (italics in the text)</li><li class=\"d0\">His new practice: look at the <b>OT context</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people treat quotes as <b>proof texts</b> and gloss over</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul brings ideas actually <b>in that text</b> to bear</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no chapters and verses</b> → quote first line, expects <b>context recalled</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2484.68,"lv":3,"h":"Habakkuk 2:1-5 read","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads <b>Habakkuk 2:1-5</b></li><li class=\"d1\">watch on the rock; <b>write the vision on a tablet</b>; yet for an <b>appointed time</b>; <b>wait for it</b>, it will surely come</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2:4</b>: if one shrinks back, my soul has no pleasure in him; the <b>righteous shall live by my faith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>drunk, scoffer, boaster</b> accomplish nothing - gathers to himself <b>all the nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Note last verse: <b>God's plan to bring in all peoples</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quote sets up themes for the <b>whole epistle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Vision is for <b>the end</b> - write it down and pass it on</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>that time is now</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2632.08,"lv":3,"h":"Faith is faithfulness, not Luther's reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Contrast: <b>righteous faithful</b> vs. one who <b>shrinks back</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Luther</b>: \"righteous will live by faith, <b>not works</b>\" - the big Lutheran line</li><li class=\"d0\">But Habakkuk describes the shrinker: <b>drunk, scoffer, boaster</b> = <b>sinful behaviors</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>living by faith = living righteously</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sifting happens <b>at the end</b>, when God gathers <b>all the nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So the group before the nations come in = <b>Israel / Judeans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some <b>faithful</b>; some <b>shrink back, fall away</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God brings in people from <b>all nations</b>, joins them to the <b>faithful remnant</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2780.94,"lv":3,"h":"Judgment among the Jewish people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul quotes the <b>whole idea</b>, will keep returning to it</li><li class=\"d0\">Judgment has happened among Jews</li><li class=\"d1\">righteous = those who accepted <b>Jesus as Messiah</b>; faith</li><li class=\"d1\">unrighteous shrank back, fell away</li><li class=\"d1\">nations called in to <b>replace</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">→ \"almost the <b>exact opposite</b> of how Luther interpreted it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2828.77,"lv":3,"h":"\"From faith to faith\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul can be a <b>confusing writer</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John's Gospel</b> parallel: recognize Jesus or not</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you don't hear my voice because you're not <b>my sheep</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if you knew the Father, you would know me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Faithful Jews hear gospel → accept → faith <b>moves to faith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness to God extends to <b>faithfulness to Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Those who shrank back in Habakkuk don't respond - <b>no faith to begin with</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2916.38,"lv":3,"h":"Why Paul can just cite Habakkuk","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"That's a lot, three lines\" - Paul writes to people who know <b>Habakkuk on the watchtower</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They heard Habakkuk in the <b>yearly cycle of readings</b> in the synagogue, and <b>preached</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like his reference to the <b>Samaritan woman</b> - read and preached one Sunday every year</li><li class=\"d0\">Some of this in our <b>liturgics</b>: an <b>ode of the canon in Matins</b> about Habakkuk on his watchtower</li></ul>"},{"t":3032.51,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: neglected prophets and Habakkuk","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Isaiah big; <b>Ezekiel</b> weird, less quoted; <b>Habakkuk</b> mostly ignored</li><li class=\"d0\">Twelve prophets neglected: <b>Obadiah, Joel, Amos, Hosea, Zephaniah</b> - Jonah read for stories</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Obadiah</b> = oracles against <b>Edom</b>, about his own period</li><li class=\"d0\">Habakkuk = vision of something <b>not yet happened</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews read it as <b>Messianic Age</b> in the future; early Christians: \"it's about <b>now</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prophet</b> = one who <b>speaks for God</b>, not just predicts the future</li><li class=\"d1\">God speaks about <b>future, present, and past</b></li></ul>"}]});
